Yes, I alluded to this earlier. But Hermes developed a strong
connection with medicine through his connection to learning. It may
have started with the alchemists, but it makes sense for scientific
medicine. Especially pharmacological medicine; even in the Odyssey,
Hermes gives Odysseus an herb to help protect him.

Someone mentioned that Apollo might make sense as a god of healing
because he fathered Aesculapius. I'd forgotten this, but some stories
have Apollo give Hermes the caduceus in exchange for a flute or lyre.
On a more esoteric note, we might view the caduceus as one snake of
life and one snake of death (not to be confused with good and evil). In
which case, the Medical Department of the United States Army may have
known what it was doing when it adopted the symbol in 1902.



